module gunbc.devboot.vertical_receipt

import std.types { String, CommitSha, NonEmptyStr }
import std.content_hash { Sha256Digest, sha256_hex_digest }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }

data devboot_vertical_receipt_note: String = "THE EXECUTION EVIDENCE FOR THE DEVBOOT VERTICAL, IN ONE TYPED HOME RATHER THAN IN A PULL REQUEST BODY. What ran, at which head, which subject it produced and which bytes came back are facts about an execution; a review comment is not a carrier, and a rendered receipt file committed as text would be prose the substrate cannot read. This module follows gunbc.native_witness_transition_receipt, which exists for the same reason and whose exclusions are copied here deliberately.\\n\\nWHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT STORED. No timings. The producer took roughly four minutes and the reusing caller two seconds on one host with a warm cargo target directory, and both numbers are properties of that host at that moment rather than of this service -- a transcribed duration is stale immediately and no oracle in this repository could catch it. The DURABLE claim is the ordering, which is structural: the second caller did not build, because an artifact already existed for its subject. No counts either: two callers and two materializations are the shape of the run, not a measurement to ratchet, and a stored integer beside them would be a second representation of what the identities below already say.\\n\\nWHAT IS STORED IS IDENTITY, because identity is what stays true. The subject ref token, the artifact object id and the artifact's sha256 are exactly reproducible from the same tree at the same settings, and they are what a later reader needs in order to check this claim rather than believe it."

type DevbootVerticalReceipt {
  evaluated_head: CommitSha
  subject_ref_token: NonEmptyStr
  artifact_object_id: NonEmptyStr
  artifact_sha256: Sha256Digest
  producer_entry: DeclarationRef
  consumer_entry: DeclarationRef
}

data devboot_vertical_standing_note: String = "THE STANDING IS DERIVED, AND IT DELIBERATELY REPORTS THE WEAKER CLAIM. This vertical ran once, by hand, through the ordinary CLI. Nothing in the floor executes it, no cadence schedules it, and a cargo build of this size could not sit on a per-PR path without blowing the witness cost budget -- so the honest standing is manual execution, not enrolled coverage.\\n\\nSaying so structurally matters more than saying it in prose. A receipt that merely recorded success would read, to anyone scanning for coverage, exactly like a receipt backed by something that re-runs -- the rung inflation DESIGN section 4b names, where a class is described at a rung above the one its evidence establishes. Here the arm itself carries the absence, so a consumer asking whether this behaviour is continuously exercised receives no.\\n\\nBOTH ARMS ARE ADMISSIBLE. Manual execution is real evidence: the binaries were built, stored, fetched, verified and run. It is simply not evidence that they still are, and the two must not be spelled the same way.\\n\\ndissolve-on: an enrolled consumer on a cadence that can afford a real build -- the same lane as the witness cost envelope. At that point the second arm gains its inhabitant and cites the consumer that produces it."

type DevbootVerticalStanding
  = VerticalExecutedManuallyOnce
  | VerticalExecutedUnderEnrolledConsumer { consumer: DeclarationRef }

fn devboot_vertical_standing() -> DevbootVerticalStanding {
  VerticalExecutedManuallyOnce
}

data devboot_vertical_receipt_inhabitant_note: String = "THE ONE INHABITANT. Two callers submitted the same subject against a freshly initialized bare repository; the first produced and the second reused the first's artifact without building. Two consumers then materialized those bytes independently, verified them by sha256 -- a digest whose preimage is the content alone, so the store cannot satisfy it by agreeing with itself -- and executed the result, both exiting zero.\\n\\nThe artifact object id is the address inside the store and the sha256 is the independent verification; neither is derivable from the other, which is why both are carried. The subject token is the ref name segment under which that artifact is found, and it carries its hash family so two digests from different families can never name one ref."

